1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,320
You type example.com, and a page appears.

2
00:00:03,725 --> 00:00:06,235
Computers don't find each
other by name, though.

3
00:00:06,495 --> 00:00:08,525
They use numbers called IP addresses.

4
00:00:09,783 --> 00:00:15,373
So before the page can load, your browser
needs the IP address of example.com.

5
00:00:16,383 --> 00:00:18,933
First it checks if it
already knows the answer.

6
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:23,753
The browser and your operating system
both keep recent answers in a cache.

7
00:00:24,917 --> 00:00:28,327
If neither of them knows it,
the browser asks a resolver.

8
00:00:28,672 --> 00:00:31,382
Your internet provider usually runs one,

9
00:00:31,592 --> 00:00:35,222
and there are public ones like 1.1.1.1.

10
00:00:36,217 --> 00:00:38,307
The resolver starts with a root server.

11
00:00:38,677 --> 00:00:40,647
The root server doesn't know the address,

12
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:43,247
but it knows where the
dot com servers are.

13
00:00:44,217 --> 00:00:46,647
The dot com servers don't know it either.

14
00:00:46,862 --> 00:00:50,612
They send the resolver to the
name server of example.com.

15
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:53,480
That name server has the answer.

16
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:55,910
It sends the IP address back,

17
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:59,250
and the resolver saves it for
the next time someone asks.

18
00:01:00,417 --> 00:01:02,887
That makes four questions
to find one address.

19
00:01:03,272 --> 00:01:05,522
It usually takes a fraction of a second.

